.TH LIBNETD 3 "" "Peios" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
libnetd \- netd client and configuration library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <peios/netd.h>

cc program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs netd)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libnetd.so.1
provides daemon status, renewal and reconciliation requests, DNS snapshot
subscriptions, and validation of hostnames, profiles, and interface policies.
The public declarations and structure layouts are in
.BR peios/netd.h
and
.BR peios/netd-types.h .
.PP
Configuration operations use the kernel policy engine snapshot recorded in
.BR vendor/kernel/ORIGIN.json .
They evaluate supplied configuration without modifying the registry or
changing network state.
Daemon operations use the existing netd control socket and its access rules.
.SH OWNERSHIP
Each operation returns an owned result handle, including on failure.
Call
.B peios_netd_result_error
before inspecting the result payload.
Zero means success; other values are POSIX error numbers.
The functions return errors through the result rather than setting
.BR errno .
.PP
Call
.B peios_netd_result_free
exactly once for every result.
Pointers returned by result getters are borrowed views and remain valid
until the owning result is freed.
Copy data that must outlive the result.
Input spans must point to valid storage for the duration of the call.
.PP
A successful subscription call also transfers a separate subscription handle.
Free it with
.BR peios_netd_subscription_free .
Result destruction does not destroy the subscription.
.SH TIMEOUTS
Daemon operations accept a positive timeout in milliseconds.
The deadline covers connection, request transmission, and response reception.
The library does not replay failed requests.
.PP
A subscription timeout retains a partial frame for the next call.
Other receive errors close the connection; subsequent reads return
.BR ENOTCONN .
Replies larger than the protocol frame limit are rejected.
.SH THREAD SAFETY
Result views are immutable and can be read concurrently while the result
remains alive.
Synchronize destruction with every reader.
Use one caller at a time for each subscription, including destruction.
.SH ERRORS
Malformed input, invalid UTF-8, invalid option tags, and excessive input
sizes produce an error result.
An allocation failure aborts the process.
Passing dangling pointers, invalid handles, or a span larger than its
allocated storage violates the C interface contract.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR net (1),
.BR netd (8)
